.TITLE "tar"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
tar is a command used to bundle files into a single archive.

It is commonly used on __Unix-like systems__.
You can think of it like packing multiple files into one container.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
tar -cvf archive.tar file.txt

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**tar -cvf** creates a new archive.

Here, __tar__ is the tool.
__-c__ creates a new archive.
__-v__ shows progress.
__-f__ specifies the file name.

archive.tar is the output file.
file.txt is the file being added.

This packs the file into a single archive.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
tar -xvf archive.tar

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**tar -xvf** extracts files from an archive.

Here, __tar__ is the tool.
__-x__ extracts files.
__-v__ shows progress.
__-f__ specifies the archive file.

archive.tar is the file being extracted.

This unpacks all files from the archive.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
tar -tvf archive.tar

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**tar -tvf** lists the contents of an archive.

Here, __tar__ is the tool.
__-t__ lists files.
__-v__ shows details.
__-f__ specifies the archive file.

This lets you check what is inside before extracting.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
tar helps bundle and extract files easily.
Always list contents with tar -tvf before extracting unknown archives.
